Report an item too long for the Windows command line on its own.
When quoting pushed a command line past the Windows limit, xargs ran
the items that fit and carried the rest into the next command. An item
whose quoted form passed the limit by itself was carried forever: the
next command was left with no items, which xargs takes to mean the end
of input, so it stopped there with status 0, silently dropping that
item and everything after it. If the item came first and -r was not
given, the command first ran once with no items.

Report such an item as too long, as xargs already does for an item
that does not fit in its buffer.

---
 findutils/xargs.c |    7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/findutils/xargs.c
+++ b/findutils/xargs.c
@@ -950,7 +950,8 @@
 		/* Check if quoting expands the command line.  If it does we
 		 * truncate args[] and preserve the tail for processing later.
 		 * With -I the args come from a single input line and form one
-		 * command, which can't be split. */
+		 * command, which can't be split.  Nor can an arg that is too
+		 * long on its own. */
 		args = G.args;
 		if (quote) {
  skip_read:
@@ -958,7 +959,9 @@
 			for (i = initial_idx; args[i]; i++) {
 				n_chars += quote_len(args[i]) + 1;
 				if (n_chars > WIN32_MAX_CHARS) {
-					if (opt & (OPT_REPLSTR | OPT_REPLSTR1))
+					if (i == initial_idx
+					 || (opt & (OPT_REPLSTR | OPT_REPLSTR1))
+					)
 						bb_simple_error_msg_and_die("argument line too long");
 					tail = args + i;
 					saved_arg = *tail;
